## The replicated figures

<!-- ONE loop over the recipe's own `figures:` block — never a hand-written list of stems and
     captions, which drifts from the recipe the moment a figure is added or renamed. Each
     caption is the figure's `Figure.description` (original, public-facing prose describing OUR
     reproduction: never the paper's caption, never the A/B framing). `output: asis` prints raw
     markdown, so the bold lead renders and the description may use LaTeX ($I_0$) -- keep it
     LaTeX + ASCII, never Unicode. The A/B composition, the copyright gate and the staging all
     live in tvbo's `report_figure`; no report writes its own `ab()`. -->
```{python}
#| output: asis
def status_callout(fig):
    """The figure's verdict, derived from the status of every target it carries.

    Three colours, no emoji (callout-icon:false in _quarto.yml): note=reproduced,
    warning=missing (data/target unavailable), important=attempted and short of its criterion.
    """
    by = {}
    for r in figure_targets(fig, TARGET_ROWS):
        by.setdefault(r["Status"].strip(), []).append(r["ID"])
    if not by:
        return ""
    met, partial, out = by.get("met", []), by.get("partial", []), by.get("out", [])
    kind = "important" if partial else "warning" if out and not met else "note"
    said = []
    if met:
        said.append(f"{', '.join(met)} met")
    if partial:
        said.append(f"{', '.join(partial)} attempted and short of its criterion")
    if out:
        said.append(f"{', '.join(out)} out of scope by declaration")
    return f"::: {{.callout-{kind}}}\n{'; '.join(said)}.\n:::\n"

for n, fig in enumerate(figures_in_paper_order(STUDY.figures), 1):
    print(f"### {figure_title(fig)}\n")
    theirs, credit = original(fig)
    staged = report_figure(FIGS / f"{fig.name}.png", theirs, STAGE,
                           credit=credit, cleared=CLEARED)
    if staged is None:
        print(f"::: {{.callout-warning}}\n`{fig.name}.png` is not rendered — "
              f"run `tvbo run <Study>.yaml`.\n:::\n")
        continue
    print(f"![**Fig {n}.** {figure_caption(fig)}](_figures/{staged.name})"
          f"{{#fig-{fig.name.replace('_', '-')} width=100%}}\n")
    print(status_callout(fig))
```

# Discussion

<Interpret. Lead with what reproduced and why it is robust (scale/seed-invariant, across
several read-outs). Then any negative result: state the mechanism/cause and its downstream
consequences (often one modelling fact explains several panel mismatches — say so rather
than listing them as independent failures). Then the reproduction-vs-replication framing
(below) and the accepted limitations.>

**Reproduction vs. replication.** In the NASEM terminology [@NASEM2019] *reproduction* re-runs
the same code on the same data; *replication* re-derives a paper's findings with **independent
methods and independently-sourced data** — which is what this is. Separate **mechanism-level**
targets (a sign / pattern / ordering that any reasonable input reproduces) from **decimal-level**
targets (a number needing the paper's exact input): the former reproduce; the latter are capped by
unavailable inputs and stated as limitations, never claimed as matches.

## Replication scorecard {#sec-scorecard}

<!-- The whole scorecard is READ from docs/analysis/targets.md, where every criterion was
     written before the first run: the tally, the per-target table, the shortfall prose and each
     figure's status callout all come from that one reading. Nothing here is typed.
     `Scorecard` owns the vocabulary: TIER (core | extended -- how central the target is) is
     independent of OUTCOME (met | short | out | blocked). Never reuse an outcome word as a tier;
     the same targets then appear in a tier row AND an outcome column and the tally crosses
     itself. Tables are wrapped in `crossref_div` so a COMPUTED caption gets a real "Table N"
     number -- the `tbl-cap` cell option only takes a literal string. -->
```{python}
#| label: scorecard
#| output: asis
SC = Scorecard(ROOT / "docs/analysis/targets.md")

print(crossref_div("tbl-tally", SC.tally_table(),
    "Replication targets by **tier** and **outcome**, each counted once. *Tier*: **core** "
    "carries one of the paper's headline claims, **extended** a supporting one. The three ways "
    "of falling short are not interchangeable -- **short of criterion** is attempted and missed "
    "(the only failure of this replication), **out of scope** is declared unattempted, **input "
    "unobtainable** is a data gap. From `docs/analysis/targets.md`."))

print(crossref_div("tbl-targets", SC.target_table(),
    f"All {len(SC.rows)} declared targets with the figure each supports, its tier, its fidelity "
    f"(*dec* = scored against the authors' own numbers, *mech* = against the shape of their "
    f"claim) and its outcome."))
```

